INTRODUCTION AND THESIS OUTLINE
1.1 tiltrotor history and wind tunnel test campaigns
Tilting rotor aircraft, or tiltrotors, with their outstanding ability to take off and land vertically like helicopters and, at the same time, to achieve high speeds up to twice those of helicopters in forward flight when operating like conventional turboprop aircraft, represents one of the few successful examples of advanced vertical lift configurations. After a long development phase that encompassed few experimental aircraft that successfully made it to flight noticeably the Transcendental Model 1-G (a tilting rotor concept) shown in Fig. 1a, which flew about 100 hours without ever completing a full conversion [109] and the Bell XV-3 (a tilting rotor configuration), shown in Fig. 1b, in the 1950s [51], and the Bell XV-15 (a tilting rotor/nacelle configuration) in the 1980s [109] shown in Fig. 2a, unlike other less fortunate technology demonstrators, the concept finally proved its soundness with the Bell-Boeing V-22, shown in Fig. 2b (a tilting rotor/nacelle configuration) [168], a military aircraft that after an almost 25 years long development phase, has been operated by the US Marine Corps with an increasing reward for the last 15 years, and is entering service for carrier onboard delivery in the US Navy. Remaining in the military arena, the Bell V-280 Valor, shown in Fig. 3a, (a tilting rotor configuration), after a test campaign started in 2017, is currently being evaluated by the US Army for its Future Long-Range Assault Aircraft (FLRAA) program [58]. However, with then Bell-Agusta and now Leonardo AW609,shown in Fig. 3b, (a tilting rotor/nacelle configuration) [144] about to become operational after a long and thorough development [63], the tiltrotor design appears to be mature enough to also enter the civil air transport market [1]. Nevertheless, tiltrotor design remains a rather challenging engineering task, considering the various operating conditions and multipurpose missions that are expected to be accomplished by this complex type of aircraft. In particular, the problem of assessing whirl flutter stability limits is at the same time fundamental and challenging. Whirl flutter is an aeroelastic stability phenomenon that affects both turboprop and tiltrotor aircraft [34]. When a rotor mounted on a flexible structure rotates, the normal vibration modes associated with the supporting structure may interact with the precession motion of the rotor.
