When its motion is perturbed, each point on the rotor axis of rotation draws paths about its reference position. This motion changes the way each rotor blade is affected by the incoming airspeed, correspondingly altering the overall aerodynamic loads. At the verge of whirl flutter, when this phenomenon is triggered, perturbations result in a periodic orbit. The resulting forces can cause divergence in the response of the system, which represents a true aeroelastic instability [165]. Today, although key aspects of the phenomenon in tiltrotor aircraft are understood, the ability to predict with high computational efficiency it is still limited, despite the availability of sophisticated aeroelastic analysis tools. The difficulty lies in its dependence on several factors, including geometric design, structural properties, actuator dynamics, and aerodynamic interaction between different components, which can all contribute to the whirl flutter characteristics in ways that are not always intuitive. The problem may be exacerbated when unconventional configurations are considered, as is the case of propeller- and rotor-driven advanced air mobility concepts [95]. Aeroelastic testing, especially when it comes to stability and flutter, can be extremely dangerous. For this reason, wind tunnel testing is needed to understand phenomena and increase the level of trust in predictions in support of their mandatory verification in flight. As a consequence, the possibility to validate numerical predictions using experimental data is of paramount importance from an industry point of view. Wind-tunnel testing of tiltrotor configurations, and its use for validation and correlation with numerical predictions,have been widely used for tiltrotor development. Due to the need to aeroelastically scale also the wing portion of the model, of extreme miniaturization of the rotor consider, for example, the need to reproduce in detail rather complex blade root attachments and pitch control mechanisms, aeroelastic wind-tunnel models of tiltrotor aircraft can be even more complex than helicopter ones. Several wind-tunnel models have been developed to explore the concept of whirl flutter in prop- and tiltrotor configurations. However, specific wind tunnel tests of configurations that were intended to be tested in flight date only back to the early development of the V-22 when Bell designed and manufactured a 1/5-scale semi-span model and used it for wind tunnel tests and correlation with the simulation capabilities available at that time [158]. Eventually, this model evolved in the Wing and Rotor Aeroelastic Test System (WRATS) 4, which has been in use for several years to explore various aspects of tiltrotor aeroelasticity, including aeroelastic tailoring of the wing [45, 139], several parametric studies (e.g., in [155]), active control for flutter suppression [70, 101], an interesting four-blade, articulated soft-inplane configuration for hub load reduction [140, 141], and several nu


