1.2 introduction to whirl flutter phenomenon
In airplane mode, under high flow conditions and when there are small variations in flow, considerable differences in load distribution cause the nacelle and the wing structure to bend or twist. Motiondependent forces can then result in greater wing motion and pylon and hub motion, initiating a repetitive cycle of aeroelastic oscillations, which can, under certain circumstances, ultimately lead to structural failure.
1.2.1 2-DOF model
Whirl flutter is a very complex type of instability. Although more and more comprehensive models have been developed over the years, a very simplified representation can still provide a basic understanding of the phenomenon. The first description proposed consists of a truly rigid propeller on a pylon with pitch and yaw degrees of freedom. A detailed discussion is presented in [164], and the representation of the model is shown in Figure 7.


