In particular, while in the non-rotating case the pitch and yaw modes can be excited independently, this is not true for the rotating propeller, for which the gyroscopic action introduces a coupling effect. The modes then become characterized by a whirl or precession mode, with reference to how the hub is with respect to the static thrust axis. In this case, even for the symmetric stiffness properties of the system, the expected frequencies for the whirl modes are different. The forward whirl, defined as occurring in the same direction of propeller rotation, manifests itself at higher frequency, whereas the


1.2 introduction to whirl flutter phenomenon 9
backward whirl, defined as opposed to propeller rotation, occurs at lower frequency. When these whirl modes produce a change in the angle of attack of the blade element, the aerodynamic forces generated are those that could cause the instability mechanism. At airstream velocities V < Vcrit the mechanism is stable, so a disturbance causes the hub to trace
a spiral path that converges to the initial equilibrium position, in cases where V > Vcrit a divergent condition occurs. For a rigid propeller
configuration, the instability occurs in the backward-whirl mode. When analyzing the separate contributions in terms of propeller aerodynamics, a significant impact is given by the yaw moment due to pitch Mz(θ) or the pitch moment due to yaw My(ψ), which generates a cross-stiffness (destabilizing) moment. In the backward whirl mode, this driving moment is resisted by the aerodynamic and structural damping moments, so that oscillations may become neutrally stable when the contributions of driving and resisting terms are exactly counterbalanced. Several studies and experimental test campaigns conducted on this type of configuration highlighted that the most important parameters found to influence the critical velocity at which whirl flutter occurs are stiffness, damping, pivot location, and propeller advance ratio. The main effect and role of each term can be summarized as shown in Fig. 9.
Figure 9: Effects of Parameters on 2-dof Whirl Flutter Model [164]
In particular, in relation to parameter variations, it is interesting to note that:
• In terms of stiffness, a system having equal pitch and yaw stiffness is significantly more subject to flutter when compared to a case where one stiffness is greatly reduced
• In terms of damping, an increased damping is significantly stabilizing
• In terms of pivot location, moving the pivot aft is significantly stabilizing
The 2-dof model presented, often referred to as the classical whirl flutter model, is mainly included for historical reasons and due to the


