The discussed model was applicable to the case of axial flight and included a modal representation for the wing, considering a total of nine degrees of freedom (6 for the three-bladed rotor system and 3 fundamental wing modes). The analytical results obtained correlated well with the full-scale test results and the model was then extended to include elastic blade characteristics and helicopter and conversion modes of operation, effectively forming the basic tiltrotor model theory for the complete CAMRAD rotorcraft code [32, 88]. Although this review of theoretical models is by no means meant to be exhaustive, it was of interest to discuss some of the modeling choices and typical procedures involved. In general, also for more sophisticated models, as discussed in [164], it is very convenient to adopt a two-phase approach, where the main components are analyzed separately and then the complete aircraft behavior is examined:
1. In the first phase, the major components should be studied individually, for example, by considering a flexible propellernacelle system mounted on a rigid wing structure or a flexible wing with the assumption that the contribution of the nacellepowerplant can be treated as rigidly attached to the wing
2. In the second phase, the equations of motion of each component should be coupled together.
1.3 impact of technology
In this Section, a brief overview of the contributions of the major tiltrotor components is presented. The considerations and findings of the work by Nixon [139], [140] and Acree [9], [6], [7], [8] are outlined on the impact of technology and design options on aeroelastic stability.
1.3.1 Rotor
1.3.1.1 Hub Concept
Possible solutions to the design of the rotor hub include the use of:
• gimballed or hinged configurations
• hingeless configurations
Two different hub concepts, mounted on the WRATS model for airplane mode testing, are shown in Fig. 4. The gimballed solution is currently the preferred approach and common practice and has been adopted for the XV-15, V-22, V-280, and AW609. Due to the kinematic constraints of the configuration, gimballed rotors do not scale well to a number of blades greater than three.


12 introduction and thesis outline
The semi-articulated solution, for example, that proposed by Bell Helicopters for the Army VGART program, having no gimbal but employing a standard blade flap hinge and elastomeric bearing for lag motion, was specifically conceived for a better scalability and growth potential by allowing the use of more than three blades. The hingeless solution, having no blade flapping, lead-lag hinges, or rotor-flapping gimbal, contains fewer parts and would allow for higher helicopter mode pitch and yaw control moments, but is cause of higher loads for blade, hub, and main transmission, providing considerable penalties from a weight perspective and also regarding the life of the structural components. The hingeless hub has not received as much attention as the gimbal counterpart, but it is acknowledged that it seems to be less susceptible to whirl flutter. In relation to the type of hub concept adopted, it is common to perform a distinction between soft-in-plane and stiff-in-plane configurations, in cases where the ratio between the in-plane natural frequency and main rotor harmonic is respectively below or above 1. Stiff-in-plane rotors are not affected by concerns for ground resonance in hover and generally provide higher whirl flutter boundaries and adequate damping margins at subcritical speeds. However, particularly during maneuvers, very high in-plane dynamic loads may develop. However, several studies including [140] have highlighted that softin-plane systems are beneficial from a load perspective, as they produce significantly lower dynamic loads, but provide reduced damping margins and stability boundaries compared to their stiff-in-plane counterparts under the same operating conditions.
1.3.1.2 Rotor Couplings
pitch-flap coupling The kinematic coupling between blade flapping and pitch is called δ3. This pitch-flap coupling creates an aerodynamic spring force that moves the flap frequency away from the rotation frequency (1P) and thus away from a resonant condition. Following common convention, a positive δ3 implies a nose-down pitching for upward flapping. Even if counter-intuitive, this type of coupling is destabilizing for some modes, typically lag modes, as it creates an aerodynamic-based increase in flapping stiffness, leading to an eventual mode coalescence as the collective is increased with airspeed. For this reason, a negative value for δ3 is adopted in the conventional tiltrotor design. The typical scheme for pitch-flap coupling is shown in Fig. 12 for a gimballed configuration for which an offset pitch horn is used. It should also be noted that a negative δ3 is as effective as a positive δ3 in reducing the maximum transient flapping response and that the potential range of values actually implementable from a practical point of view is limited by the blade spacing.


1.3 impact of technology 13
Figure 12: Pitch-Flap Coupling [6]
pitch-lag coupling Pitch-Lag coupling is strongly influenced by rotor precone and control system stiffness. In relation to precone, the value of βp, as shown in Figure 13, is chosen to improve blade loads in helicopter mode, when the disk loading is high and large blade bending moments arise. In this condition, the precone angle allows a component of centrifugal force that counteracts the contribution of the lift bending moment, so the net moment is reduced compared to the case without precone.
Figure 13: Precone Effect [139]
However, an excessive precone leads to a destabilizing effect in airplane mode, due to the role of a generated pitch-lag coupling. In cruise condition, disk loading is considerably reduced with respect to hover, and the centrifugal force component is reduced as well, owing to the lower rotor speed. The significant imbalance that is then created causes a torsion moment about the blade inboard section proportional to the lag bending deflection, as shown in Fig. 14, generating a destabilizing coupling effect. The mentioned pitch-lag coupling is also affected to some extent by the stiffness of the control system. Studies conducted in relation to the stability of a rigid hub have shown that decreasing the stiffness of the control system had a destabilizing effect, while analyses of a flexed hub configuration demonstrated that this negative effect is less severe or has little impact on stability considerations [155]. The flexed


14 introduction and thesis outline
Figure 14: Torsion about Inboard Sections due to Lag Motion [139]
hub is designed specifically to allow the rotor to flatten out in cruise flight, reducing the design precone angle βp by the angle βh and consequently reducing the total pitch-lag coupling.
1.3.1.3 Blade Design
The blade aerodynamic design is what primarily determines the twist of the blade and the definition of the planform. The twist and chord distributions are typically based on a compromise between the figure of merit in hover and propeller efficiency in airplane mode, with chord selection subject to low-speed maneuvering capability requirements. The blade has an intermediate twist between that of a conventional rotor and that of a propeller and is characterized by an airfoil selection which depends on the radial location. The adopted profiles are thicker at the root for structure build-up, are selected to provide a maximum lift-to-drag ratio in the middle, and are thin outboard. Blade taper improves hover efficiency, but reduces the propeller one. Regarding blade design for improved stability margins, several studies have discussed the impact of variations in the center of gravity of the blade and the aerodynamic center along the blade. In particular, Acree [9] has shown that small forward offsets of the aerodynamic center with respect to the elastic and pitch axis, obtained by moving the airfoil quarter-chord, considerably increased stability boundaries, while small forward offsets of the center of gravity had a similar but less pronounced effect. The blade parametric variations were distributed from root to tip along four radial stations, and the offset contributions were analyzed separately, which also showed that the effect was largest in the outboard blade segments. The findings of the work were interesting because they proved that an increased chordwise distance between the center of gravity of the blade and the aerodynamic center, the classical approach adopted to increase stability for an isolated airfoil, was also applicable to the entire rotor-wing dynamic system, given the highly coupled nature of the whirl-mode instability. An important aspect that emerged from the investigation was related to the role of the proposed variations in the mean and oscillatory rotor loads. While the potential to define variations that increase stability should be considered, it should always be verified


1.3 impact of technology 15
that such modifications do not have a severe adverse effect on loads in both helicopter and airplane modes. Acree [6] later extended the focus to other design modifications, with an approach that clearly grew from previous efforts. Specifically, in relation to blade design, both the sweep of the outboard sections and the moving of the balance weights at the tip ahead of the elastic axis were evaluated. Figure 15 shows the case for the considered swept blade modification derived from the baseline V-22 rotor.
Figure 15: V-22 Blade Modifications - Sweep and Tip Mass Offset [6]
The research revealed how the most favorable combinations greatly increased the damping of the critical modes in terms of stability, fully stabilizing the rotor. In a more general analysis of advanced geometry blades on whirl flutter stability, the tip anhedral has also been shown to increase the speed of flutter, while the planform taper, the tip dihedral and the forward sweep have proven detrimental on the stability margins [178].
1.3.1.4 Operational Speed
An important driver to consider in the structural design phase, especially in relation to the strong impact it has on blade dynamics, is the operational speed of the rotor. The value adopted is generally defined according to different considerations. In airplane mode, it is about 80% the value in helicopter mode. In this wide range of operating conditions, it is of paramount importance to guarantee adequate separation of the fundamental flap, lag, and torsion frequencies of the blade from harmonics associated with the operating speeds of the rotor during hover and cruise and to avoid any destabilizing frequency crossings.


16 introduction and thesis outline
While it is true that a reduction in rotor speed reduces the forces that drive whirl flutter, the issues related to frequency placement and modal coupling are potentially exacerbated. Ultimately, it is clear that rotor system design must always consider the important trade-offs that exist between the necessary required stability margins and blade loads that arise when adopting one solution rather than the other.
1.3.2 Wing
The current practice in tiltrotor design is to employ a high-mounted wing with a tilting engine nacelle configuration. Some studies have also been performed considering low-wing configurations, for example in relation to the NASA Large Civil Tiltrotor (LCTR) program. Low-mounted wing designs have associated requirements that include fixed engines with tilting shafts, longer rotor shafts or extreme dihedral for fuselage clearance in hover, and hingeless rotors for adequate pitch control in hover. As the hingeless rotor tends to be less susceptible to whirl flutter, this wing configuration would potentially allow for a less strict torsional stiffness requirement. However, a wing with a tilting shaft and fixed engines will have different maximum torsional design loads with respect to the high-wing tilting engine nacelle counterpart because of the different offset between the rotor thrust vector and the wing center of gravity [7]. Conventional high-wing configurations are very stiff and thick (with a thickness-to-chord ratio around 0.23) and are characterized by a limited aspect ratio. Although a reduced wing thickness would be beneficial from a high-speed performance point of view, by allowing for a great reduction in profile drag, it would drastically limit the possibility of meeting the aeroelastic stability requirements. For this reason, the authors, including Nixon [139], have proposed solutions based on aeroelastic wing modifications to introduce beneficial couplings without incurring excessive weight penalties. Composite tailoring, in particular, would provide the opportunity to increase stability margins when considering a thin-wing configuration by modifying mode shapes and frequency placement of the main wing modes. However, in this case, the internal structure of the wings must still be able to accommodate the transmission cross shaft and the fuel tanks, so a thickness reduction driven by performance considerations alone may not always be possible. In addition to the dynamics of the wings itself, the stability margins of the whirl flutter are greatly affected by the characteristics of the associated wing components, such as the stiffness of the transmission adapter, mast and pylon, conversion spindle, and downstop [139]. Among these, the downstop plays a particularly important role, which is the mechanism that allows locking the pylon to the wing


1.3 impact of technology 17
for a tiltrotor in airplane mode configuration. When not engaged, the stiffness of the attachment is governed by that of the conversion actuator only and is greatly reduced compared to the downstop-on configuration. The contribution of the drive system should also be considered for a proper modeling approach, as it has a significant impact on both the pylon/wing system and on the proprotor aeroelastic behavior. In relation to the effects of the drivetrain, Acree [6] considered the impact of eliminating the drivetrain entirely or eliminating only the flexibility of the drivetrain (thus considering a rigid drivetrain model). In both cases, it appeared that the presence of the drive train caused a significant loss of predicted damping on symmetric and antisymmetric wing beamwise-bending modes. Figure 16 shows the comparison between the results considering the symmetric wing bending mode (SWB).
Figure 16: Drive-train Effect [6]
As neglecting the presence of the drive system leads to non-conservative results in terms of damping, this contribution should definitely be taken into account in both the design and the testing phase.
1.3.3 Additional Considerations
From the discussion presented, it is clear that the rotor and wing cannot be designed independently. Although it is true that performance goals for hover and cruise determine the initial rotor design and the set wing area and maximum thickness, loads associated with the sizing condition determine the structural design. The need to account for the potential onset of dynamic instabilities should be considered early in the design, as performance in airplane mode may be limited by whirl flutter rather than by aerodynamics or installed power. A posteriori corrections of the design may not always be feasible and are always cause of cost penalties and schedule delays, and should thus be avoided. In general, there are several options for improving tiltrotor aeroelastic stability margins, and it is either possible to consider passive or active techniques, as well as a combination of both.


18 introduction and thesis outline
In relation to active techniques, a very promising solution consists of the adoption of active stability augmentation system [128]. However, flutter suppression systems are not accepted, at least up to date, from a regulatory point of view: the tiltrotor should be free (with margins) from any instability phenomena in the entire flight envelope. In relation to passive techniques, a summary of the most important aspects and considerations is presented in view of the design options discussed in previous sections and the findings from [75]:
1. The rotor design parameters that have the greatest influence on whirl flutter stability are pitch-flap and pitch-lag couplings
2. The wing design parameters that have the greatest influence on whirl flutter stability are wing vertical bending and torsion stiffness, as well as vertical bending-torsion coupling
3. Modest changes in key design parameters for rotor and wing may provide a significant flutter speed increase
4. Generally, flutter speed has shown a greater sensitivity towards variations in rotor rather than wing design
Ultimately, having identified whirl flutter as a critical design requirement for the tiltrotor configuration, it is evident that the tools adopted in the design phase should be able to capture the behavior of parametric modifications on key design parameters and allow to easily analyze each component both separately and as part of the integrated vehicle. From a modeling point of view, tools that provide the capability to use anisotropic beam theory are often the only viable option to reproduce the most important coupling effects in the design without leading to excessive computational times. This is especially true when the goal is to assess the impact of different design options at an early design stage, as excessive computational time for each analysis would drastically hinder the applicability of the approach.
1.4 tiltrotor-rotor pilot couplings
Rotorcraft-pilot-couplings (RPC) [149] are events that are caused by the pilot’s erroneous perception of vehicle dynamics. RPC phenomena can be divided into two families:
• Pilot-Induced Oscillations (PIO): when the pilot inadvertently excites divergent vehicle oscillations by applying control inputs that are in the wrong direction or have phase lag with aircraft motion.
• Pilot-Assisted Oscillations (PAOs) are the result of involuntary control inputs by the pilot in the loop that may destabilize the


1.4 tiltrotor-rotor pilot couplings 19
aircraft due to inadvertent man-machine couplings. Generally, for PAO to occur, an involuntary involvement of the pilot due to his biodynamic response to vibrations is required.
PAOs usually occur in the frequency range between 2 − 8 Hz. In PAOs, airframe vibrations are transferred through the biodynamic feed-through (BDFT) to the control input, which in turn causes new structural vibrations. A block diagram of the pilot-aicraft close loop is shown in Fig. 17.
