Although PAO phenomena have also been identified in fixed-wing aircraft, it is in rotorcraft that they showed a very high incidence [55, 147, 148]. Rotorcrafts are more susceptible to those high-frequency occurrences since their high-order dynamics play a prominent role, as shown in Fig. 18. The control inputs given by the pilots through the cockpit inceptors are transmitted through the swashplate to the blades, thus affecting the rotor flapping and control moments transferred to the airframe. The rotating mechanism can generate, for any control at frequency ω, oscillations at ω ± Ω, where Ω is the angular speed of the rotor. Thus, low-frequency pilot inputs generate high-frequency blade excitation. An interesting analysis of the effects of pilot BDFT on the V-22 was presented in [145], while a more comprehensive analysis of all PAO phenomena that can occur on a tiltrotor, without considering the interaction with FCS, can be found in [135]. Recently, in the work of Muscarello and Quaranta [136], the structural coupling problems for tiltrotors are investigated, considering not only the interaction of the flight control system with the flexible structure, but also the potentially adverse effects on aeroservoelastic stability. The investigation is focused on the analysis of side effects that could appear at high speed in the flight regime of aircrafts, where the whirl flutter boundaries may be significantly reduced. A detailed linearized tiltrotor model, representative of the Bell XV-15 and of a flight control system, has been built and joined with a linearized pilot biodynamic model acting on the power lever and on the center stick. The model has been developed in MASST [120] that connects the substructures using a Craig-Bampton component mode synthesis approach [47].


