1.5 thesis objective and outline
The main objective of this work is to develop, validate, and evaluate the aeroelastic and aeromechanical stability limits of a tiltrotor aircraft using a comprehensive approach. The final goal is to provide a set of validated, open-source, and multipurpose tools to the scientific and industrial community that can simulate in a single environment all of the ingredients that participate in the aeroelastic loop: this objective is motivated by the lack of such framework in the current literature, which is a gap to be filled to enable considering whirl flutter and also phenomena such as PIO and PAO early in the design process of future tiltrotors while accounting for effects such as the detailed resolution of aerodynamic interactions, general flexible structures, and coupling with the pilot dynamics. For what concerns the aerodynamic part, two models are adopted: a low-fidelity model based on pure 2D strip theory and a mid-fidelity model based on the vortex particle method developed in open source code DUST. The structural part and the control part are modeled using the multibody code MBDyn. The communication between the two software is handled by the open-source coupling library preCICE. Finally, the completely non-linear biomechanical model of the pilot is coupled with the aeroservoelastic tiltrotor model: this model is developed in MBDyn. Thanks to the large amount of public data [4, 25, 31, 61, 62] the XV15 tiltrotor aircraft equipped with Advanced Technology Blade (ATB ) is chosen as the base model. This work is organized as follows.
• Chapter 2: In this chapter, the used and developed software tools are presented from a theoretical perspective. A brief theoretical overview of the MBDyn code is given, and instead, a detailed theoretical overview of the DUST code is provided. Finally, the tight coupling between the two codes is presented, focusing on the implementation of the interpolation scheme that is needed to correctly deform the aerodynamic mesh and send the aerodynamic loads to the structure.
Contribution: The non-linear vortex lattice elements are implemented, then the coupling between DUST and MBDyn is developed improving, in particular, the interpolation scheme and the wake shedding.


