SOFTWARE TOOLS USED/DEVELOPED
The design of complex rotary-wing vehicles, such as tiltrotors, represents a challenge for engineers and scientists. Being able to perform complete aeroelastic simulations considering the coupling of rotarywing aerodynamics with the structural dynamics of the vehicle is essential for the development of novel aircraft configurations [17]. Sophisticated numerical tools have been developed to support the analysis of rather different operating flight conditions typical of VTOL vehicles with increasing level of detail. In fact, substantial effort has been made in recent years to develop sophisticated structural dynamics codes (CSD) that were used effectively for rotorcraft applications (see, e.g., references [20, 89, 207]). In detail, the structural dynamics of the rotary wings vehicles was typically investigated using the multibody approach [69, 160], which takes into account the non-linear dynamics of the interconnected rigid and flexible bodies that represent the components of the aircraft during transients. The multibody approach was also used to investigate aeroelastic phenomena, especially in flight in airplane mode, where whirl flutter instabilities can occur [127]. A particular effort in this research field was made at the Politecnico di Milano, where, starting in the 1990s, a free general purpose multibody software called MBDyn (https:
//public.gitlab.polimi.it/DAER/mbdyn) was developed, with the
aim of gaining autonomous modeling capabilities of generic problems related to the dynamics of complex aeroelastic systems, specifically rotorcraft and tiltrotors [119]. A tiltrotor flight mission is characterized by vertical take-off and landing, typical of helicopters, and by a cruise flight condition typical of fixed-wing airplanes. The transition between the two flight conditions occurs through the so-called conversion maneuver, in which the proprotor-nacelle system is tilted. Therefore, an accurate model for the evaluation of unstable loads that occur during transients must be considered for a correct analysis of the complex dynamics characterizing these vehicles. However, multibody solvers, such as MBDyn, are typically equipped with simplified aerodynamics models based on Blade Element/Momentum Theory (BE/MT). This type of approach does not consider aerodynamic interference between the rotors and the actual geometry of the lifting surfaces, which can lead to misrepresentation of aerodynamic loads and loss of information related to periodic actions. Accurate computation of unsteady aerodynamic loads for rotary wing vehicles was the main goal of several research groups that developed, in recent decades, different high fidelity Computational Fluid
