3.3.2 Propeller
As second test case, the results of the Workshop for Integrated Propeller Prediction (WIPP) [79] are compared with the DUST simulations. The WIPP model represents a useful test case for validating aerodynamic codes, since an intense experimental campaign was carried out, the results of which constitute a powered wind tunnel test database. The tests were carried out at the Lockheed Martin Low Speed Wind Tunnel (LSWT) in Marietta, Georgia, USA, as part of the NASA/Armstrong X-57 research program [79]. The model consisted of a 40.5% scale semi-span wing designed for the wind tunnel test campaign to be mounted on the LSWT external balance. The span, from the tunnel floor to the top of the nacelle, is equal to 1.772 m, with a root chord of 0.295 m and a tip chord of 0.218 m. The wing has a sweep at the leading edge of 1.9◦. The model incorporates a non-metric boundary layer splitter plate 0.163 m high, which is mounted on the tunnel floor. The model is equipped with an existing C-130 four-blade propeller with a diameter of 0.411 m and a blade pitch angle of 38◦. The propeller was also extensively used in previous activities to test a 10% scale C-130 aircraft model. The propeller is mounted on a nacelle at the tip of the wing. The nacelle is 0.613 m long with a maximum diameter of 0.12 m. The accurate geometries


